# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Twitch Radio. The four Twitch values below are required. Authorizing the
# bot account is a separate one-time step — see the README.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# From https://dev.twitch.tv/console/apps — register an application there
# (OAuth Redirect URL: http://localhost:4343/oauth/callback), Client ID and
# a generated Client Secret both come from that app's page.
TWITCH_CLIENT_ID=
TWITCH_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Numeric Twitch user IDs, not usernames — digits only, nothing else (not
# "Twitch ID:1536026185", just "1536026185" — Helix rejects that with a bare
# "Bad Identifiers" error that doesn't say which field is wrong; validated at
# startup with a clearer message than that). BOT_ID is the account the bot
# chats as (recommend a dedicated account, made a moderator in your
# channel); OWNER_ID is the broadcaster/channel the bot joins. Look either
# up from a username at
# https://www.streamweasels.com/tools/convert-twitch-username-to-user-id/
TWITCH_BOT_ID=
TWITCH_OWNER_ID=

# Command prefix in Twitch chat
# TWITCH_PREFIX=!

# Feature toggles (radio autoplay, chat filters) live in data/toggles.json,
# adjustable live via !toggle in chat or the /settings page — nothing to
# set here. One exception: actually deleting a message the link/caps filter
# flags (filter_delete_enabled) needs an extra OAuth scope beyond the setup
# below (moderator:manage:chat_messages) — see chatbot.py's module
# docstring for the exact URL. Without that scope the toggle just stays
# warn-only; nothing breaks either way.

# MP3 bitrate for the audio stream this service serves (see below). 128 is
# fine for speech+music; raise toward 192-256 if it sounds thin.
# AUDIO_BITRATE_KBPS=128

# This service doesn't stream to Twitch itself — it serves the queue as a
# live MP3 stream at /stream.mp3, plus a visual /overlay page, which you add
# as sources in your own OBS:
#   - Media Source   -> https://<your-domain>/stream.mp3
#   - Browser Source -> https://<your-domain>/overlay
#
# Keep TWITCH_NOWPLAYING_HOST at 127.0.0.1. To reach it from another machine,
# put Caddy in front (deploy/setup.sh installs and configures it) rather than
# binding 0.0.0.0. See the README's "Publishing with Caddy" section.
# TWITCH_NOWPLAYING_HOST=127.0.0.1
# TWITCH_NOWPLAYING_PORT=8098

# Password for the /settings sign-in page. Either the password itself or a
# hash — `python bot.py --hash-password` prints one. With no password,
# /settings and /blocklist.json only work from this machine, never through a
# reverse proxy.
TWITCH_SETTINGS_PASSWORD=

# Escape hatch: with no password, `true` lets anyone who can reach the
# server use /settings. Only for a network you fully trust.
# TWITCH_SETTINGS_ALLOW_OPEN=false

# Sign-in length in hours (1-168), and days for "keep me signed in" (0-365,
# 0 hides the checkbox). Restarting the bot signs everyone out.
# TWITCH_SESSION_HOURS=12
# TWITCH_SESSION_REMEMBER_DAYS=30

# Reverse proxies whose X-Forwarded-For/-Proto/-Host headers are believed
# (comma-separated IPs or CIDR ranges). The default covers Caddy on this
# machine; only change it if the proxy runs somewhere else.
# TWITCH_TRUSTED_PROXIES=127.0.0.1/32,::1/128

# Public base URL, no trailing slash — e.g. https://radio.example.com. Set
# this and !commands in chat links viewers to the /commands page; unset, it
# falls back to the terse in-chat listing. setup.sh fills this in when it
# sets up Caddy.
# TWITCH_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=

# Extra emote sources the chat overlay (/chat-overlay) draws as images, on top
# of Twitch's own emotes: any comma-separated subset of 7tv, bttv, ffz,
# cheermotes — or "none". Unset = all four. The bot downloads their emote
# lists at startup and every 30 minutes; a source that's down just shows its
# emotes as text.
# TWITCH_CHAT_EMOTE_SOURCES=7tv,bttv,ffz,cheermotes

# Filenames under data/ (gitignored, and the only directory the systemd
# unit's ReadWritePaths allows writes to) — no reason to change these
# unless you're running more than one instance out of one data/ directory.
# TWITCH_TOKEN_FILE=twitch_tokens.json
# TWITCH_TUNABLES_FILE=tunables.json
# TWITCH_BLOCKLIST_FILE=blocklist.json
# TWITCH_SPECS_FILE=specs.json
# TWITCH_TOGGLES_FILE=toggles.json
# TWITCH_DB_FILE=community.db

# By default the queue advances on its own real-time clock whether or not
# anything is actually connected to /stream.mp3 — if OBS crashes or a scene
# reloads, whatever played in the meantime is just gone when it reconnects.
# Set this to true to hold off starting a new track until someone's
# listening again (a track already playing when the last listener
# disconnects always finishes normally — this only affects track
# boundaries, not mid-track).
# PAUSE_QUEUE_WHEN_NO_LISTENERS=false

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# yt-dlp
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Path to a cookies.txt file (Netscape format). On a residential connection,
# leave this unset — ordinary public search/URL requests don't need it, and
# yt-dlp has a known, recurring failure mode ("The page needs to be
# reloaded") that specifically shows up MORE often with cookies enabled, not
# less — see https://github.com/yt-dlp/yt-dlp/issues/16212 and
# https://github.com/yt-dlp/yt-dlp/issues/17389.
#
# On a CLOUD VM, this is different: YouTube blocks datacenter IPs more
# aggressively and you'll likely hit "Sign in to confirm you're not a bot"
# even on an ordinary search. Setting this (a real cookies.txt exported from
# a logged-in browser session — an empty/placeholder file makes things
# worse, not better) is the simplest fix. See the README for a more
# durable, cookie-free alternative (a local PO-token provider) if you don't
# want to deal with periodically refreshing this file.
#
# MUST be a path under data/ — that's the only directory this service's
# systemd sandbox (ReadWritePaths, in deploy/twitch-radio.service) allows it
# to write to. yt-dlp tries to save this file back on every single !sr, not
# just when it changes, so pointing this anywhere else (including the repo
# root, e.g. a bare "cookies.txt") will crash every song request with a
# read-only-filesystem error. Checked at startup either way — a misconfigured
# path here fails loudly before the service starts, not silently on the
# first !sr.
# YTDLP_COOKIES_FILE=data/cookies.txt

# URL of a local bgutil-ytdlp-pot-provider instance (see README), if you've
# set one up as the cookie-free alternative above. Leave unset otherwise —
# yt-dlp works fine without one, just more likely to get blocked on a
# datacenter IP.
# YTDLP_POT_PROVIDER_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4416

# yt-dlp needs an external JavaScript runtime to fully support YouTube (Deno,
# Node, Bun, or QuickJS — see https://github.com/yt-dlp/yt-dlp/wiki/EJS).
# Leave unset and yt-dlp auto-detects a `deno` binary on PATH — deploy/setup.sh
# installs Deno system-wide for exactly this. Only set this to pin a
# specific binary instead. If you switch YTDLP_JS_RUNTIME_NAME to node,
# it needs to be Node >=22 — Ubuntu's own apt "nodejs" package is almost
# always older than that; use https://github.com/nodesource/distributions
# or nvm, not a bare `apt install nodejs`.
# YTDLP_JS_RUNTIME_PATH=
#
# Which runtime YTDLP_JS_RUNTIME_PATH points to. Only matters if that's set.
# Defaults to "deno" since that's what setup.sh installs; change this if
# you're pinning a Node/Bun/QuickJS binary instead.
# YTDLP_JS_RUNTIME_NAME=deno

# Comma-separated override for yt-dlp's YouTube player_client selection.
# Leave unset for the built-in default: when YTDLP_COOKIES_FILE is set,
# this defaults to "web_embedded,web" — both already yt-dlp's own top
# choices for an authenticated session, just without ever falling through
# to tv_downgraded, a third default candidate with a currently-open bug
# that returns "The page needs to be reloaded" (yt-dlp#17389). Without
# cookies, yt-dlp's own default is used untouched. YouTube changes what
# works here often enough that this is worth checking directly if requests
# start failing: https://github.com/yt-dlp/yt-dlp/wiki/EJS
#
# If YTDLP_COOKIES_FILE is set, at least one client here must support
# cookies — android/android_vr/ios/visionos/tv_simply all reject cookie
# auth outright, so a cookies+mobile-only-clients combination fails every
# single request. Checked at startup; invalid client names (typos) are
# just warned about, not fatal, since yt-dlp itself only skips those.
# YTDLP_PLAYER_CLIENT=

# How long (seconds) a resolved song is reused instead of running a second
# full yt-dlp extraction. Every !sr resolves once in chat (to confirm/queue
# it) and again right before it actually plays — this avoids paying for
# that twice for anything near the front of the queue, without
# reusing a resolve so old it risks missing a video that went live/private/
# deleted in the meantime. 0 disables caching entirely. Range 0-3600.
# Where yt-dlp actually runs. "process" (the default) uses a pool of
# long-lived child processes: extraction stops competing with the audio
# feed loop for the GIL, and a request that wedges can be killed rather
# than occupying a worker until restart. "thread" is the old in-process
# pool, kept as an escape hatch. See the README section "Out-of-process
# extraction".
# YTDLP_WORKER_MODE=process

# YTDLP_CACHE_TTL_SECONDS=300

# How many concurrent yt-dlp extractions this service runs at once. There's
# no sibling feature to protect from contention anymore (that's the whole
# point of this being a separate service) — 2 is a reasonable default for a
# single-viewer-facing radio; raise it if !sr gets busy.
# YTDLP_CONCURRENCY=2
# YTDLP_EXTRACT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=45

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Logging
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# LOG_LEVEL=INFO
# LOG_TO_FILE=true
